The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
throw new IOException(e);
```
Reason:
This line in the original code indiscriminately wraps all exceptions thrown during remote procedure calls in a generic `IOException`, leading to the loss of specific exception types. Callers that expected to handle specific exceptions could not distinguish between them, as they were all downgraded to a single type.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
Throwable cause = e.getCause();
if (cause instanceof IOException) {
    throw (IOException) cause;
} else {
    throw new IOException(cause);
}
```
Reason:
These lines in the patch check the underlying cause of the `ExecutionException`. If the cause is an `IOException`, it is thrown as is, preserving its type. If the cause is a different exception, it is wrapped in an `IOException`, which maintains some of the original exception's information. This approach allows callers to handle different exception types appropriately, restoring the intended behavior.

